WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.791 --> 00:00:12.992
Hey there, Little Fox readers!

2
00:00:13.852 --> 00:00:15.253
It's me, Jake, again.

3
00:00:16.213 --> 00:00:20.455
Last week I watched a cool movie that takes place at a temple deep in the jungle.

4
00:00:21.235 --> 00:00:22.276
The next day Mr.

5
00:00:22.356 --> 00:00:25.637
Fay said we'd be learning about a new landmark called Angkor Wat.

6
00:00:26.478 --> 00:00:29.679
And when he showed us a picture of it, I recognized it right away.

7
00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:32.120
It was the temple from the movie!

8
00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.923
Angkor Wat is an ancient Buddhist temple located in Angkor, Cambodia.

9
00:00:37.843 --> 00:00:43.426
It's famous for its carved sandstone towers and for being one of the largest religious structures in the

10
00:00:43.527 --> 00:00:44.287
whole world.

11
00:00:45.748 --> 00:00:50.651
Angkor Wat is also considered a really important work of architecture from the Khmer Empire.

12
00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:57.275
Mr. Fay said that this powerful empire covered most of Southeast Asia and it flourished from the ninth

13
00:00:57.435 --> 00:00:58.656
to thirteenth centuries.

14
00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:06.004
That was when Khmer rulers built many cities and villages, along with canals, roads, palaces, and temples.

15
00:01:07.065 --> 00:01:12.329
In the twelfth century, a king named Suryavarman II ordered the construction of Angkor Wat.

16
00:01:13.229 --> 00:01:18.133
It was built to serve as both a temple for the Hindu god Vishnu and a massive tomb

17
00:01:18.173 --> 00:01:19.073
for the king himself.

18
00:01:20.134 --> 00:01:25.438
Over time Buddhism spread, and by the thirteenth century, it was the official religion of the Khmers.

19
00:01:26.378 --> 00:01:30.681
Many of the Khmer temples, including Angkor Wat, were converted to Buddhist temples.

20
00:01:32.302 --> 00:01:38.304
Nobody is quite sure why, but sometime in the fifteenth century, the Khmers abandoned Angkor and moved their

21
00:01:38.344 --> 00:01:39.745
capital to Phnom Penh.

22
00:01:40.705 --> 00:01:46.208
Except for a small number of Buddhist monks who stayed behind, Angkor was forgotten until a French explorer,

23
00:01:46.448 --> 00:01:48.469
Henri Mouhot, traveled to the site.

24
00:01:49.429 --> 00:01:54.132
Mouhot wrote about Angkor and created some ink drawings of the temples that got other people interested in

25
00:01:54.192 --> 00:01:54.652
them too.

26
00:01:55.572 --> 00:02:01.456
Today hundreds of the Khmer temples can still be seen in the area known as Angkor Archaeological Park.

27
00:02:02.457 --> 00:02:05.098
Angkor Wat is definitely the most famous temple there.

28
00:02:06.079 --> 00:02:06.619
And as Mr.

29
00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:11.683
Fay told us, it's also the biggest temple, covering about 2.6 square kilometers.

30
00:02:12.763 --> 00:02:16.226
In fact, its name, Angkor Wat, means "city temple."

31
00:02:17.026 --> 00:02:18.827
So it is one huge place!

32
00:02:20.429 --> 00:02:25.352
Angkor Wat is Cambodia's busiest tourist attraction, with close to a million visitors each year.

33
00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:28.994
It's also an important visiting place for Buddhist monks.

34
00:02:29.975 --> 00:02:32.798
If you're ever lucky enough to go, try to get there early.

35
00:02:33.258 --> 00:02:37.361
I heard that it's pretty awesome to see the sun rise from behind the temple's tall towers!

36
00:02:38.362 --> 00:02:43.867
To enter the temple, visitors first have to cross an enormous moat that surrounds the entire complex.

37
00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:48.671
Today this moat is filled with lily pads and lotus blossoms, but Mr.

38
00:02:48.711 --> 00:02:51.433
Fay said it probably once had crocodiles too.

39
00:02:52.414 --> 00:02:54.356
I bet nobody ever went swimming in there!

40
00:02:55.744 --> 00:03:00.286
The temple itself has nine towers; each one is shaped like a giant lotus blossom.

41
00:03:01.206 --> 00:03:04.148
Four outer towers surround the five main inner ones.

42
00:03:05.168 --> 00:03:10.410
The center inner tower is the tallest of all and sits on top of the temple's main shrine.

43
00:03:11.331 --> 00:03:15.833
For most people, climbing the steep steps to the top of the temple is quite a workout.

44
00:03:16.673 --> 00:03:18.954
And it can be dangerous and slippery too.

45
00:03:19.834 --> 00:03:23.836
The temple's builders had a good reason for making this such a treacherous climb.

46
00:03:24.736 --> 00:03:29.858
The five inner towers symbolize the five peaks of Mount Meru, the home of the Hindu gods.

47
00:03:30.838 --> 00:03:35.359
Walking up those steps is supposed to be like ascending to where the gods and goddesses live!

48
00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:40.721
At the top of the steps, visitors can tour the temple's corridors and shrines.

49
00:03:41.761 --> 00:03:46.883
Many people enjoy seeing the famous bas-reliefs, or shallow carvings, in the temple walls.

50
00:03:47.963 --> 00:03:56.487
Some depict historic battles, like the Battle of Kurukshetra, which had soldiers fighting alongside elephants and horse-drawn chariots.

51
00:03:57.587 --> 00:04:02.310
Other bas-reliefs show scenes of everyday life or King Suryavarman II's court.

52
00:04:03.711 --> 00:04:08.113
The most famous bas-relief in Angkor Wat has a scene from Hindu mythology.

53
00:04:09.074 --> 00:04:13.776
It's called Churning of the Sea of Milk, and it shows gods and demons in what looks like

54
00:04:13.816 --> 00:04:15.437
an epic game of tug-of-war.

55
00:04:16.278 --> 00:04:19.700
But really they are trying to create a special potion for eternal life.

56
00:04:20.588 --> 00:04:23.911
And it isn't a rope they are tugging on—it's a giant snake!

57
00:04:24.852 --> 00:04:25.913
Pretty awesome, huh?

58
00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:31.357
Angkor Wat is still an amazing place, but it has faced some threats over the years.

59
00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:38.283
During the centuries that Angkor was abandoned, the surrounding jungle grew all around the temples, hiding them from

60
00:04:38.363 --> 00:04:44.368
sight. Today the roots of huge banyan trees are still wrapped around some of the structures at Angkor

61
00:04:44.468 --> 00:04:45.549
Archaeological Park.

62
00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.276
I like the way the giant roots look, but Mr.

63
00:04:49.356 --> 00:04:54.979
Fay said they can do serious damage over time, cracking the stone and even causing sections of the

64
00:04:55.039 --> 00:04:56.379
temples to topple over.

65
00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.862
The outer walls of Angkor Wat have also been damaged by bullet holes from all the fighting that

66
00:05:01.922 --> 00:05:05.503
took place during Cambodia's civil war in the late twentieth century.

67
00:05:06.404 --> 00:05:11.986
The fighting made the area around Angkor very dangerous, giving thieves the chance to steal valuables from the

68
00:05:12.026 --> 00:05:19.450
temples. Believe it or not, entire statues were carried away, and large sections of bas-reliefs were chiseled right

69
00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:20.370
off the walls!

70
00:05:21.911 --> 00:05:26.593
I was glad to hear that many people are working hard today to protect and restore Angkor Wat,

71
00:05:26.974 --> 00:05:29.955
along with the other temples at Angkor Archaeological Park.

72
00:05:31.016 --> 00:05:36.819
Security officers patrol the site to keep out thieves while scholars from around the world carefully repair and

73
00:05:36.859 --> 00:05:38.200
maintain the ancient ruins.

74
00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:42.702
And the people of Cambodia are still very proud of their beautiful temple.

75
00:05:43.603 --> 00:05:47.369
Angkor Wat appears on some of the country's money—and on its national flag!

76
00:05:48.270 --> 00:05:52.694
When I watched that movie the other day, I thought it was pretty cool to see spies tracking

77
00:05:52.734 --> 00:05:54.696
each other down inside an ancient temple.

78
00:05:55.597 --> 00:05:56.818
But you know what's even cooler?

79
00:05:57.759 --> 00:05:59.020
The real Angkor Wat!
